{
  "_comment": [
    "Cross-language canonical-byte vectors for the v6 signed pairing invite:",
    "a host serving BOTH routes at once - bound to an interface AND dialled out",
    "to a relay. The Rust host signs; the Kotlin client verifies. If the two",
    "canonical encodings diverge by one character, EVERY signature fails to",
    "verify - silently, on a phone, with both sides' own tests green. This file",
    "is what catches that in CI instead.",
    "",
    "v6 ALWAYS CARRIES BOTH ROUTES: addr and port as v4 does, relay_addr,",
    "relay_port and session as v5 does. A host with one route still mints v4 or",
    "v5. The phone races the routes and presents the ONE token only on the route",
    "whose pinned handshake completes first.",
    "",
    "Built from the same fixed inputs as the v4 and v5 vectors: each half is the",
    "v4 vector's LAN route plus the v5 vector's relay route under the same key.",
    "",
    "Both directions are recorded on purpose. `rust_generated` is verified by",
    "Kotlin; `kotlin_generated` is verified by Rust.",
    "",
    "Regenerate deliberately, never by hand:",
    "  cargo run -p pairing --example generate_dual_vector",
    "  (cd apk && ./gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest --tests '*InviteV6Test*' --rerun-tasks)",
    "then read KOTLIN_VECTOR lines out of the test XML system-out.",
    "",
    "Field order in canonical_utf8 is LOAD-BEARING and is not alphabetical: the",
    "LAN route where v4 has it, then the relay route in v5's order."
  ],
